<DocsLayout title="Joining" description="Join a world or server with an invite link.">
  <h2>Join with a link</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In Minecraft, open <strong>Multiplayer</strong>, then <strong>Add Server</strong> or
      <strong>Direct Connect</strong>.</li>
    <li>Paste the <code>etmc://</code> link into the address field.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Join Server</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the host chose to sign with their Minecraft account, the link shows who invited you. etmc checks that name with
    Mojang when you connect, and won't join if the host can't prove it. <strong>Session status</strong> shows when
    Mojang has confirmed them.
  </p>
  <p>
    etmc tries for a direct connection first. If that's taking too long, click <strong>Join now anyway</strong> to
    skip the wait. This can fail if the host isn't reachable yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Join from the etmc menu</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Press <kbd>G</kbd> and choose <strong>Join with a link</strong>.</li>
    <li>Paste the link, or click <strong>Paste from clipboard</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    From the title screen, etmc connects right away. From inside a world, it adds the server to your
    <strong>Multiplayer</strong> list so you can join after leaving. You can also run
    <code>/etmc join &lt;link&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>If etmc can't confirm the host</h2>
  <p>
    Your link names the host's key, and etmc connects only if the host can prove it's theirs. If it can't, etmc stops
    and tells you. Someone on the network may be posing as the host, or the host may have a new key. Ask them for a new
    link, somewhere you know it's really them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Older links don't have a key, so etmc can't check who you reached. <strong>Session status</strong> tells you when
    that's the case.
  </p>

  <h2>Check your connection</h2>
  <p>Run <code>/etmc status</code> to see how many players are connected, and how many of them directly.</p>

  <h2>Don't have a link?</h2>
  <p>
    If a server admin gave you a config URL, see <a href="/docs/connect-url">Config URLs</a>. If you know the
    network details, you can build a link with the <a href="/generator">generator</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
